module lib/hf/stab
  Orbital-rotation stability of an SCF solution, and the orbital-basis
  quantities it is built from: the orbitals of a density, the Fock matrix
  and the two-electron integrals in the orbital basis, the Hessian of the
  energy with respect to rotations of occupied orbitals into virtual ones,
  and the follow-down from a saddle point to a lower solution.

  An SCF run stops where the energy is stationary. That does not make it a
  minimum: some rotation of the orbitals may still lower the energy. This
  module computes the second derivatives of the energy along every such
  rotation and follows the lowest one down when it is negative. The module
  text in lib/hf/stab.viv gives the formulas.

stab_orbitals  @14a97589
  how   Build the Fock matrix of each density with the J/K member the
        options pick, move it into the orthonormal basis X, diagonalize it,
        and take the eigenvectors back to the basis functions. The lowest
        orbitals of each spin get the occupation flag `1`, as many as the
        setup's electron count for that spin.
  why   The analysis needs orbitals, not densities: a rotation mixes an
        occupied orbital with a virtual one. Using the eigenvectors of
        $F(D)$ gives the same orbitals the SCF driver would fill next, so at
        a converged density they are the SCF's own
        [test "water 6-31G is stable and follow-down leaves it alone, with either Hessian member"].
  p     the SCF setup: $H$, $S$, the integrals, the electron counts, the options
  D     the densities, `[nd, n, n]`: one for RHF, one per spin for UHF
  sh    D's shape
  nd    the number of densities
  n     the number of basis functions
  opts  the options, as the array the stage members read
  X     the orthogonalizer, `[n, m]`: its columns are orthonormal combinations of basis functions
  m     the number of orthonormal functions X keeps
  F     the Fock matrices of the densities, `[nd, n, n]`
  orb   the orbital pack being filled, `[nd, n + 1, m]`
  d     a density (a spin)
  V     the eigenvectors of that density's Fock matrix in the orthonormal basis

  = V    diagonalize the Fock matrix in the orthonormal basis, with the eigensolver the options pick
  = orb#2  each orbital's coefficients on the basis functions: $C = X V$
  = orb#3  the lowest orbitals are occupied, as many as the spin's electron count

stab_density  @3ab1be84
  why   The energy of a rotated set of orbitals is the energy of its
        density, so every energy this module compares is computed from here.
  orb   the orbital pack, `[nd, n + 1, m]`
  sh    its shape
  nd    the number of densities
  n     the number of basis functions
  m     the number of orbitals
  D     the densities, `[nd, n, n]`
  w     electrons per occupied orbital: `2` for one closed-shell set, `1` per spin for two
  d     a density
  C     the orbitals of density d, `[n, m]`
  Cf    C with each column times its occupation flag, so the virtual ones drop out
  p     an orbital; the occupation flag in row n leaves out the virtual ones

  $$ D_{ij} = w \sum_{p\ \text{occupied}} C_{ip}\, C_{jp} $$

stab_fock_mo  @a59cd5fc
  how   The Fock matrix of the pack's own densities, then $C^\top (F C)$.
  why   In the orbital basis the occupied-virtual block of $F$ is the
        energy gradient. At a converged density, with the orbitals
        stab_orbitals gives, $F$ is diagonal in the orbital basis and its
        diagonal holds the orbital energies (lib/hf/casida reads them from
        it).
  orb   the orbital pack
  H     the core Hamiltonian, `[n, n]`
  Gp    the packed two-electron integrals
  sh    the pack's shape
  nd    the number of densities
  n     the number of basis functions
  m     the number of orbitals
  D     the pack's densities
  F     their Fock matrices in the basis functions, `[nd, n, n]`
  Fm    the Fock matrices in the orbitals, `[nd, m, m]`
  d     a density
  C     the orbitals of density d, `[n, m]`

  $$ F^{\mathrm{MO}} = C^\top F\, C $$

stab_mo_eri  @866393e1
  how   Four quarter transformations, one index at a time: sum the first
        index against the orbitals, then the second, the third and the
        fourth. Each step is a sum over one index of the basis functions,
        so each costs about $m\, n^4$ multiplications instead of the
        $m^4 n^4$ of the direct sum.
  why   The Hessian's formulas and the Casida matrices (lib/hf/casida) are
        written with integrals over orbitals. Dense tensors are simple, and
        they fit in memory for small molecules like water in 6-31G.
  G     $(\mu\nu|\lambda\sigma)$  the integrals over basis functions, dense, `[n, n, n, n]`
  orb   the orbital pack
  s     the spin of the orbitals in the first pair
  t     the spin of the orbitals in the second pair
  sh    the pack's shape
  n     the number of basis functions
  m     the number of orbitals
  A     $(p\nu|\lambda\sigma)$  the first index transformed, `[m, n, n, n]`
  B     $(pq|\lambda\sigma)$  the first two transformed, `[m, m, n, n]`
  C3    $(pq|r\sigma)$  three transformed, `[m, m, m, n]`
  M     $(pq|rs)$  the integrals over orbitals, `[m, m, m, m]`
  p     the first orbital
  q     the second orbital
  r     the third orbital
  u     the fourth orbital
  i     a basis function summed against the first orbital
  j     a basis function
  k     a basis function
  l     a basis function
  v     one sum, over the index being transformed

  $$ (pq|rs) = \sum_{\mu\nu\lambda\sigma} C_{\mu p}\, C_{\nu q}\, C_{\lambda r}\, C_{\sigma s}\, (\mu\nu|\lambda\sigma) $$
  = A#2   the first index: $(p\nu|\lambda\sigma) = \sum_\mu C_{\mu p} (\mu\nu|\lambda\sigma)$
  = B#2   the second index, against the first result
  = C3#2  the third index, with the second pair's spin
  = M#2   the fourth index: the integrals over orbitals

stab_fill_block  @5bcd8d90

  = h      the Coulomb response between the two spins' rotations: the only term when the spins differ
  = f#2    on the diagonal of the occupied indices, the virtual-virtual Fock element
  = f#3    on the diagonal of the virtual indices, minus the occupied-occupied one
  = h#2    RHF: the whole closed-shell formula, four times the bracket
  = h#3    UHF, same spin: add the Fock terms and the same-spin exchange terms
  = Hs     the entry, at the block's row and column

stab_hop  @96bea3bc
  orb   the orbital pack, `[nd, n + 1, m]`
  Fm    the Fock matrices in the orbital basis, from stab_fock_mo
  Gp    the packed two-electron integrals
  idx   each spin's occupied, then virtual, orbital indices (stab_layout)
  no    each spin's number of occupied orbitals
  off   each spin's first parameter in the Hessian's layout
  kap   the rotation parameters $\kappa$
  nd    the number of spins
  n     the number of basis functions
  m     the number of orbitals
  X     the AO matrices $C_d (\kappa_d + \kappa_d^\top) C_d^\top$, `[nd, n, n]`
  JK    J and K of the X matrices
  hk    the result, $H \kappa$, in the Hessian's layout
  pref  the Fock part's factor: `4` for RHF, `2` per spin for UHF
  G     the field's response to the rotation, `[n, n]`
  C     the orbitals of spin d, `[n, m]`
  GC    $G C$
  f     the Fock part of one entry

  = X#2    each rotation parameter adds its occupied-virtual pair to the symmetric AO matrix $X_d = C_d (\kappa_d + \kappa_d^\top) C_d^\top$
  = JK     J and K of the X matrices: the response of the Coulomb and exchange fields to the rotation
  = G      RHF: the field's response, $8 (J - K/2)$
  = G#2    UHF: the Coulomb response of both spins, minus this spin's exchange
  = f#2    the Fock part, $F_{vv} \kappa$ ...
  = f#3    ... minus $\kappa F_{oo}$
  = GC     the field applied to the orbitals, $G C$; the field part of an entry is $C_a^\top (G C)_i$
  = hk#2   one entry of the Hessian times $\kappa$

stab_expm  @ddf848e2
  K      the generator, `[m, m]`
  m      its size
  nrm    the largest absolute row sum of K
  sq     how many times K was halved, and how many squarings undo it
  scale  $2^{-sq}$
  U      the identity, then the Taylor sum, then $e^K$
  T      the current Taylor term
  k      the term's order less one
  c      one squaring

  = nrm#2    the largest absolute row sum of K, a bound on its norm
  = scale#2  halve until the scaled K has row sums at most 1/4
  = T#2      the next Taylor term: the last one times the scaled K, over its order
  = U#3      add it to the sum
  = U#4      square the result, once for every halving

stab_rotate  @d92adca3

  = K#2    the antisymmetric generator: $\kappa$ at (virtual, occupied) and $-\kappa$ at (occupied, virtual)
  = out#2  the rotated orbitals, $C\, e^{tK}$, the exponential from stab_expm
  = out#3  the occupation flags, unchanged
